import
	../../html

intro-lenses html[]()
	- section "Lenses",
		- p "When dealing with an immutable record, you may want to update just one field and leave others alone."
		- keen-runnable quote
			main void()
				a thing = name: "alice", size: 1
				log change-size a, 2

			thing record(name string, size nat)

			change-size thing(a thing, new-size nat)
				name: a.name
				size: new-size

		- p quote
			Writing functions like {function "change-size"} is tedious,
			so Keen will automatically generate a {lens} for every record field.

		- keen-runnable quote
			main void()
				a thing = name: "alice", size: 1
				log set a, size, 2

			thing record(name string, size float)

		- p quote
			{set} is a function that takes a record, a lens, and the new value.
			{br}
			In this case, it uses the {function "size"} lens and sets the new size to 2.

		- aside-small "Despite the name {set}, this returns a new record without mutating the original."

		- p quote
			If the new value depends on the old value, use {modify}.

		- keen-runnable quote
			main void()
				a thing = name: "alice", size: 1
				log modify a, size, it + 1

			thing record(name string, size float)

		- p quote
			{modify} works like {set} but takes a lambda to compute the new value from the old.

	- section "Combining lenses",
		- p quote
			{var-a}{sp}{plus}{sp}{var-b} is a lens that gets / sets both {var-a} and {var-b} as a tuple.

		- keen-runnable quote
			main void()
				a r = x: 1, y: 2, z: 3
				log modify a, x + z, it.swap

			r record(x nat, y nat, z nat)

		- p quote
			{var-a}{sp}{times}{sp}{var-b} is a lens that delves into {var-a} then {var-b}.

		- keen-runnable quote
			main void()
				a hands =
					left: thumb: true, fingers: 4
					right: thumb: true, fingers: 5
				log set a, left * thumb, false

			hands record(left hand, right hand)
			hand record(thumb bool, fingers nat)

	- section "Defining lenses",
		- p quote
			{lens} is an interface (see {intro-link interfaces}) and you can define your own.

		- keen-runnable quote
			main void()
				a hands = is-left-handed: true, left: "a", right: "b"
				log modify a, dominant-hand, "\{it\}!"

			hands record(is-left-handed bool, left string, right string)

			dominant-hand (hands, string) lens()
				get: of x
					if is-left-handed x then x.left else x.right
				set: of x, value
					set x, (if is-left-handed x then left else right), value

		- p quote
			The above example is simpler using {computed-lens}.

		- keen-runnable quote
			main void()
				a hands = is-left-handed: true, left: "a", right: "b"
				log modify a, dominant-hand, "\{it\}!"

			hands record(is-left-handed bool, left string, right string)

			dominant-hand (hands, string) lens()
				computed-lens \{if is-left-handed it then left else right\}

	- section "\"then\"",
		- p quote
			The {then_} keyword provides a syntax for chaining several updates.

		- keen-runnable quote
			main void()
				log
					0 .. 10
					then to::nat[]
					then filter::nat[] it.is-even
					then map::nat[] it + 100

		- p quote
			Each {then_} must start on a new line.
			{br}
			It is followed by a function name, whose first argument is the previous line,
			then the rest of the arguments (if any).
			{br}
			This is equivalent to:

		- keen-runnable quote
			main void()
				log
					v0 = 0 .. 10
					v1 = to::nat[] v0
					v2 = filter::nat[] v1, it.is-even
					map::nat[] v2, it + 100

		- p "{then_} can be used with any function, but it's particularly suited to {set} and {modify}."

		- keen-runnable quote
			import
				keen/math/geom2d/aa-rect

			main void()
				a aa-rect32 = left: 0, right: 10, bottom: 0, top: 10
				log
					a
					then set left, 3
					then modify right, it - 3

		- p "Using {then_}{sp}{if_}, the change can be conditional."

		- keen-runnable quote
			main void()
				log foo 10, false, false
				log foo 10, false, true
				log foo 10, true, false
				log foo 10, true, true

			foo nat(a nat, incr bool, double bool)
				a
				then if incr then + 1
				then if double then * 2

		- aside-small quote
			The two {then_} keywords in {then_}{sp}{if_}{sp}{variable "incr"}{sp}{then_} have different meaning.
			The first introduces a {then_} chain, the second separates the {if_} condition from the consequence.

-plus html-node()
	std-function "keen/lens", "+"
-times html-node()
	std-function "keen/lens", "*"
-computed-lens html-node()
	std-function "keen/lens", "computed-lens"
-lens html-node()
	std-type "keen/lens", "lens"
-modify html-node()
	std-function "keen/lens", "modify"
-set html-node()
	std-function "keen/lens", "set"
